#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "4bbaf6b0-1057-5485-a3d8-2126e74fecdc" :question/math-academy-id "e-3717" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x) = x^2$ on the interval $[0,2]$." :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x)$ is a polynomial, so it is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2)$.\n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c \\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\nf'(c) = \\dfrac{f(b) - f(a)}{b-a}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(2) - f(0))/(2 - (0)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2^{2} - 0^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2}) = 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf'(c) = 2c\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c &= 2 \\\\\nc &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063959 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8756e5e-3ce4-5c02-8700-8e2f3fa81e9b" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Mean Value Theorem Statement From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "ed796d54-235b-5ec0-b73f-5396d1ef99b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-3718" :question/problem "The function $y=f(x)$ below is continuous on $[-1,4]$ and differentiable on $(-1,4)$. Complete the sentence below.\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_ \\,$.*\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/50f7d14f01067a1e593877f08d34b5b2.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given that $f(x)$ is continuous on $[a,b]$ and differentiable on $(a,b)$, the mean value theorem states the following:\n\n> *There exists a point $c \\in (a,b)$ such that\n> $f'(c) = \\dfrac {f(b) - f(a)} {b-a}$.*\n\nIn our case, we have $a = -1$ and $b=4$, so the above statement becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(c) &= (f(4) - f(-1))/(4 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6 - 2}{4 + 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n> *The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $c \\in (-1,4)$ such that* $f'(c) =\\dfrac {4}{5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063960 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "999d8587-8f15-5871-81dd-26c0c0ae063e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions Presented Graphically That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "d47e7afd-bed0-5f2b-8635-ddd1a5303837" :question/math-academy-id "e-3719" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/288/Source/Images/412e4f68cffe765264fae545418ef7fe.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$, the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{I}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that the sharp corners at $x=\\pm 2$ are not in $(-2,2)$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{II}$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-2$. So it is not continuous on $[-2,2]$, and therefore it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{III}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem. Note that although the function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2$, it is right-continuous at $x=-2$.\n- The function shown in diagram $\\textrm{IV}$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a vertical tangent at $x=0$. So, it is not differentiable at $x=0$, which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2)$. Therefore, it does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nIn conclusion, only diagrams $\\textrm{I}$ and $\\textrm{III}$ show functions that satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064036 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "92bba9e0-62db-5c96-8cbc-3cfbe8863a94" :question/math-academy-id "e-6738" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$?\n\n1. $f(x)= 3^{x} - 1$\n2. $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$\n3. $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$" :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$, the function must be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$.\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 3^{x} - 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $f(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $g(x)= x^3 - x^2 + 1$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty, \\infty)$. In particular, it is continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n- The function $h(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is not defined at $x = 0$, hence, it is not continuous at\n$x = 0$, and so it is not continuous on $[0,1]$. Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only the functions I and II satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[0,1]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064540 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88e3d067-2528-5c55-8044-490c432f2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061757} {:answer/id #uuid "d14d0ecb-4e70-50d2-9e87-9b82afa329ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2},-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061758} {:answer/id #uuid "03535dc2-ea61-532a-a4ff-534f15f9ff91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061759} {:answer/id #uuid "1a64e379-c22b-5207-a757-f06a736775f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1, 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061760} {:answer/id #uuid "6595e7f9-ad3c-5412-8705-8a501961f634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2, 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03535dc2-ea61-532a-a4ff-534f15f9ff91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061759} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e600205-c0c2-5d7a-b075-92abc52471a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061756}] :question/id #uuid "a1265601-11c0-5a35-970f-ce54ccd35f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-2341" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) = x^{2}$ on the interval $[- 8, 6]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3f4a987-4c82-55b0-aa48-923ee6c0417e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a06fd94c-099a-56f1-b59e-b5a7e9384d6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061763} {:answer/id #uuid "ad201ebd-2dbc-5477-8baa-7c3ca4313cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1, \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061764} {:answer/id #uuid "c64cd4ea-abeb-575f-a921-d270fbb7b6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}, \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061765} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a1ba38-0eb8-5581-886c-c65023583843" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "± \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061766} {:answer/id #uuid "17aee65d-af9c-5e42-b259-0fc7d84bb32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17aee65d-af9c-5e42-b259-0fc7d84bb32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061767} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74c09c3-4d6e-568b-8afc-f107ee004dc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061762}] :question/id #uuid "28c30899-c2a6-542c-9303-ca40c0edc2cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-2344" :question/problem "Find the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for\n\n$f(x) =-3\\sqrt{x} + 2$ on the interval $[0, 2]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86753037-e85f-526a-b774-8c0659f77fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062928} {:answer/id #uuid "a6100670-1055-509a-a088-058bca96263b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062929} {:answer/id #uuid "b9102e9e-13b7-5785-b166-7d6f47ee81d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062930} {:answer/id #uuid "1df6efe1-6ed3-5